from (values
  (1, 'Diagnostic: where are you now?', 'quiz', '{"questions":[{"prompt":"The core job of an operating system is best described as...","options":["managing the hardware and sharing it fairly among programs","compiling source code into machine code","rendering web pages in a browser","designing the physical processor chip"],"answer":0,"explanation":"An operating system is the layer that manages memory, the processor, and devices, then hands those resources out to running programs - see ''What an operating system does''."},{"prompt":"A process is...","options":["a single machine instruction","a running program with its own private memory","a file sitting on disk","a network packet"],"answer":1,"explanation":"A process is a program in execution, given its own address space by the operating system so it cannot stomp on other programs."},{"prompt":"Two threads inside the same process share...","options":["nothing at all","only separate processor cores","the same address space, including the heap and globals","completely separate address spaces"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Threads live inside one process and share its memory; only their stacks and registers are private - see ''Threads, concurrency, and scheduling''."},{"prompt":"A processor scheduler decides...","options":["which ready task runs next on a core","how much memory to install in the machine","how files are laid out on disk","the resolution of the display"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The scheduler picks, from the set of ready tasks, which one gets the processor next and for how long."},{"prompt":"Virtual memory lets a program...","options":["run without any processor","use an address space larger than physical memory by paging to disk","skip all permission checks","avoid the operating system entirely"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Virtual memory gives each process a large private address space, backed by physical memory plus disk when needed - see ''Memory management and virtual memory''."},{"prompt":"A lock (mutex) is used to...","options":["give one thread at a time access to shared data","raise the processor clock speed","compress files on disk","encrypt network traffic"],"answer":0,"explanation":"A mutex enforces mutual exclusion so that only one thread enters a critical section at a time."},{"prompt":"Deadlock occurs when...","options":["a program finishes normally","the disk becomes full","the processor sits idle","each thread holds a resource and waits for another, forever"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Deadlock is a cycle of waiting: everyone is stuck holding something the next one needs - see ''Synchronization''."},{"prompt":"The main job of a file system is to...","options":["organize bytes on storage into named files and directories","schedule threads on the processor","manage the graphics card","load the boot firmware"],"answer":0,"explanation":"A file system turns a flat pool of storage blocks into a named, structured tree of files and directories - see ''File systems, I/O, and devices''."}]}'::jsonb, null, 10),
  (2, 'What an operating system does, and processes', 'reading', '{"sections":[{"heading":"What you''ll be able to do","text":"Explain the three big jobs an operating system performs, describe what a process is and what lives in its address space, and outline how a process is created and how it ends. Warm-up: from the diagnostic, what makes a process different from a program?"},{"heading":"The idea","text":"An operating system sits between raw hardware and the programs people run. It has three big jobs. First, it is a resource manager: it hands out the processor, memory, and devices so that many programs can share one machine without colliding. Second, it is an abstraction layer: instead of talking to a specific disk controller, a program opens a file; instead of tracking physical memory addresses, it uses a clean private address space. Third, it is a protection boundary: it runs untrusted programs in a limited mode and reserves dangerous operations for itself. To keep these guarantees, the processor runs in two modes. User mode is restricted; kernel mode, where the operating system runs, can touch everything. A program crosses from user mode into the kernel through a system call, a controlled doorway such as ''read this file'' or ''give me more memory''. A process is a program in execution. Each process gets its own address space, laid out as code, global data, a heap that grows upward as the program asks for memory, and a stack that grows downward for function calls. The operating system also tracks per-process bookkeeping - open files, the current processor registers, and a state - in a structure often called a process control block. A process is usually in one of three states: running on a core, ready and waiting for a core, or blocked waiting for something like disk or input."},{"heading":"Worked examples","text":"Creating a process: on many systems a program makes a ''fork'' call, which produces a child process that is a near-copy of the parent, and then an ''exec'' call, which replaces the child''s memory with a new program. A shell uses exactly this pattern to launch a command: fork, then exec the command, then wait for it to finish. System call in action: when a program calls read on a file, the processor switches to kernel mode, the operating system checks that the file is open and permitted, copies bytes from a device into the program''s buffer, and switches back to user mode with a return value. State transitions: a text editor waiting for a keypress is blocked; when you press a key it becomes ready; when the scheduler picks it, it becomes running; if it then reads a large file it becomes blocked again while the disk works."},{"heading":"Your turn","text":"1) Name the three big jobs of an operating system. 2) Which mode can execute privileged instructions - user or kernel? 3) What is the mechanism a user program uses to request a service from the kernel? 4) In the fork-then-exec pattern, which call makes the copy and which loads the new program? 5) A process is waiting for data from the disk. Which of the three states is it in? Answers: 1) resource manager, abstraction layer, and protection boundary. 2) kernel mode. 3) a system call. 4) fork makes the child copy; exec loads and runs the new program image. 5) blocked."},{"heading":"Common mistakes & exit ticket","text":"Mistake: using ''program'' and ''process'' as synonyms - the program is the passive file, the process is the active execution with its own memory and state. Mistake: assuming any code can run a privileged instruction; only kernel mode may, which is why system calls exist. Exit: (a) What structure does the operating system keep per process to record its registers, state, and open files? (b) Which system call replaces a process''s memory with a new program? Answers: the process control block; exec."}]}'::jsonb, 'A program is the file on disk; a process is that program while it is running. One program can become many processes.', 14),
  (3, 'Threads, concurrency, and processor scheduling', 'reading', '{"sections":[{"heading":"What you''ll be able to do","text":"Distinguish threads from processes, explain why a context switch is needed, and compare the main scheduling policies by how they trade off responsiveness against throughput. Warm-up: two threads in one process share which parts of memory?"},{"heading":"The idea","text":"A thread is a single flow of execution. A process can contain several threads, and all threads in a process share the same address space - the same heap, globals, and open files - while each keeps its own stack and its own copy of the processor registers. That sharing makes threads cheap to create and quick to communicate, but it also means two threads can touch the same data at once, which is where bugs live (that is the next lesson). Because there are usually more ready threads than cores, the operating system time-slices: it lets a thread run for a short while, then performs a context switch, saving that thread''s registers and loading another''s. Context switches are not free, so the scheduler tries to switch often enough to feel responsive but not so often that overhead dominates. The scheduler chooses the next thread using a policy. First-come first-served runs tasks in arrival order - simple, but one long task delays everyone behind it. Round robin gives each ready task a fixed time slice in turn, which keeps the system responsive. Shortest-job-first runs the shortest task next, which minimizes average waiting time but needs to guess task lengths and can starve long tasks. Priority scheduling runs the highest-priority ready task; to stop low-priority tasks from waiting forever, systems raise a task''s priority the longer it waits, a fix called aging. Two words describe the trade: throughput is how much work finishes per unit time, and latency (or response time) is how quickly a given task gets attention."},{"heading":"Worked examples","text":"Threads versus processes: a browser might run each tab as a thread so tabs share cached data cheaply, or as a separate process so one crashing tab cannot corrupt the others - the choice trades sharing against isolation. First-come first-served pain: tasks arrive needing 10, 1, and 1 units. In arrival order the two short tasks wait 10 and 11 units. Run the short ones first and their waits drop to 0 and 1, cutting average wait sharply - that is the shortest-job-first intuition. Round robin: with a 2-unit slice and three equal tasks, the scheduler rotates through them so all three make steady progress and none is ignored, at the cost of more context switches. Aging: a low-priority backup job keeps losing to interactive work; as it waits, its priority is nudged up until it finally runs, so it never starves."},{"heading":"Your turn","text":"1) Name one thing threads in a process share and one thing each thread keeps private. 2) What does a context switch save and restore? 3) Which policy can make one long task delay every task behind it? 4) Which policy minimizes average waiting time but risks starving long tasks? 5) What is aging and which problem does it solve? Answers: 1) they share the address space (heap, globals, open files); each keeps its own stack and registers. 2) it saves the current thread''s registers and loads the next thread''s registers. 3) first-come first-served. 4) shortest-job-first. 5) aging gradually raises a waiting task''s priority; it solves starvation under priority scheduling."},{"heading":"Common mistakes & exit ticket","text":"Mistake: thinking concurrency requires multiple cores - a single core interleaves threads by switching between them. Mistake: assuming shortest-job-first is always best; it needs to know or estimate job lengths and can starve long jobs without aging. Exit: (a) In round robin, what fixed quantity does each ready task receive in turn? (b) Which metric measures work finished per unit time, throughput or latency? Answers: a fixed time slice (quantum); throughput."}]}'::jsonb, 'Concurrency means tasks make progress in overlapping time; parallelism means they literally run at the same instant on different cores. You can have concurrency on a single core.', 15),
  (4, 'Checkpoint 1', 'quiz', '{"questions":[{"prompt":"Which statement about kernel mode is correct?","options":["It is where untrusted user programs run","It can execute privileged instructions that user mode cannot","It is only used during boot","It is slower than user mode by design"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Kernel mode is the privileged mode the operating system runs in; user programs must cross into it through system calls."},{"prompt":"In the fork-then-exec pattern, the exec call...","options":["duplicates the current process","replaces the process''s memory with a new program image","terminates the parent process","creates a new thread"],"answer":1,"explanation":"fork makes the child copy; exec loads and starts running a different program in that process."},{"prompt":"Threads within one process each have their own...","options":["heap","global variables","stack and registers","open file table"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Threads share the address space (heap, globals, open files) but keep private stacks and register sets."},{"prompt":"A context switch primarily involves...","options":["saving one thread''s registers and loading another''s","reformatting the disk","increasing the clock speed","freeing all memory"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Switching threads means storing the running thread''s processor state and restoring the next thread''s state."},{"prompt":"Round robin scheduling gives each ready task...","options":["priority based on its length","a fixed time slice in rotation","the processor until it blocks","service only after all others finish"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Round robin rotates a fixed quantum among ready tasks, keeping the system responsive."},{"prompt":"Aging is introduced mainly to prevent...","options":["deadlock","starvation of low-priority tasks","context switches","page faults"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Aging raises a waiting task''s priority over time so it eventually runs instead of starving."}]}'::jsonb, null, 9),
  (5, 'Memory management and virtual memory', 'reading', '{"sections":[{"heading":"What you''ll be able to do","text":"Explain why virtual memory exists, translate a virtual address into a physical one using pages, describe what a page fault is, and reason about how a replacement policy chooses which page to evict. Warm-up: why should one process be unable to read another process''s memory?"},{"heading":"The idea","text":"If programs used physical addresses directly, two programs could overwrite each other, and a program could not use more memory than the machine physically has. Virtual memory fixes both. Each process is given its own large virtual address space, starting from address zero as far as it can tell. Memory is split into fixed-size chunks called pages (physical memory is split into equal-size page frames). A per-process page table maps each virtual page number to a physical frame, and it also stores permission bits, such as read-only or no-execute. To translate an address, the hardware splits it into a page number and an offset within the page: it looks the page number up in the page table to find the frame, then adds the offset. Because doing this lookup on every access would be slow, a small hardware cache called the translation lookaside buffer, or TLB, remembers recent translations. When a program touches a page that is not currently in physical memory, the hardware raises a page fault; the operating system pauses the program, fetches the page from disk into a free frame, updates the page table, and resumes. Physical memory is finite, so when it fills, the operating system must evict a page to make room. Which one? The ideal is to evict the page that will be used furthest in the future, but that requires knowing the future, so real systems approximate it. Least-recently-used evicts the page unused for the longest time, betting that the recent past predicts the near future. If a program is given too few frames, it can thrash - spending almost all its time paging in and out instead of computing."},{"heading":"Worked examples","text":"Address translation: suppose pages are 4096 bytes, so the low 12 bits of an address are the offset (since 2 to the 12th is 4096). A virtual address in page number 5 with offset 100 is looked up: if the page table says virtual page 5 maps to physical frame 9, the physical address is frame 9''s base plus 100. Change only the frame in the table and the same virtual address now points somewhere else in physical memory - that indirection is the whole trick. Protection: mark a page read-only in the table, and a write to it triggers a fault the operating system can turn into an error, which is how it stops a program from corrupting code. Replacement: frames hold pages A, B, C (loaded in that order) and the program now needs page D. Under least-recently-used, if the recent access order was A, C, B, then A is the least recently used and is evicted for D. Page-fault cost: because a disk fetch is enormously slower than a memory access, even a small fault rate can dominate running time, which is why keeping the working set in memory matters."},{"heading":"Your turn","text":"1) What two problems does virtual memory solve at once? 2) With 4096-byte pages, how many bits of an address are the offset? 3) What does the translation lookaside buffer cache? 4) A program accesses a page not in physical memory. What is this event called, and who handles it? 5) Under least-recently-used with frames holding A, B, C and recent use order B, A, C, which page is evicted when a new page arrives? Answers: 1) it isolates processes from each other and lets a program use an address space larger than physical memory. 2) 12 bits, because 2 to the 12th equals 4096. 3) recent virtual-to-physical address translations. 4) a page fault; the operating system handles it by loading the page from disk. 5) B, since it was used least recently."},{"heading":"Common mistakes & exit ticket","text":"Mistake: confusing a page (a fixed-size chunk of the virtual address space) with the page table (the map from virtual pages to physical frames). Mistake: believing more paging is always fine - past a point a program thrashes, doing almost nothing but moving pages. Exit: (a) What hardware structure speeds up address translation? (b) What is it called when a program spends most of its time paging instead of computing? Answers: the translation lookaside buffer (TLB); thrashing."}]}'::jsonb, 'A virtual address is what the program uses; a physical address is where the byte really lives. The page table is the map between them.', 15),
  (6, 'Synchronization: locks, semaphores, and deadlock', 'reading', '{"sections":[{"heading":"What you''ll be able to do","text":"Identify a race condition, protect shared data with a lock, use a semaphore to coordinate threads, and recognize the four conditions that together cause deadlock. Warm-up: if two threads both run ''count = count + 1'' at the same time, why might count only go up by one?"},{"heading":"The idea","text":"When threads share data, timing can corrupt it. The statement ''count = count + 1'' is really three steps: read count, add one, write it back. If two threads interleave those steps, both can read the same old value and both write back the same new value, losing an update. That timing-dependent bug is a race condition, and the stretch of code that touches shared data is a critical section. The goal is mutual exclusion: only one thread in the critical section at a time. A lock, or mutex, provides it - a thread acquires the lock before the critical section and releases it after; anyone else must wait. A semaphore is a more general counter with two operations: wait, which decrements and blocks if the count is zero, and signal, which increments and may wake a waiter. A semaphore initialized to 1 behaves like a lock; initialized to N it lets up to N threads through, which is handy for a pool of identical resources. Semaphores also coordinate ordering, as in the producer-consumer pattern, where one semaphore counts filled slots and another counts empty slots so a consumer never reads an empty buffer. Locks solve races but introduce a new hazard: deadlock, where threads wait on each other in a cycle and none proceeds. Deadlock needs four conditions at once: mutual exclusion (a resource is held exclusively), hold-and-wait (a thread holds one resource while waiting for another), no preemption (a resource cannot be forcibly taken away), and circular wait (a cycle of threads each waiting on the next). Break any one condition and deadlock cannot occur; a common, practical fix is to break circular wait by making every thread acquire locks in the same global order."},{"heading":"Worked examples","text":"Lost update: threads T1 and T2 both do count = count + 1 starting at 0. Both read 0, both compute 1, both write 1 - final value 1, not 2. Wrap the read-modify-write in a lock and the two updates serialize to give 2. Semaphore as a resource pool: a semaphore initialized to 3 guards three printers; the first three threads pass wait and print, a fourth blocks until one signals on finishing. Producer-consumer: ''empty'' starts at the buffer size and ''full'' starts at 0; a producer waits on empty then signals full, a consumer waits on full then signals empty, so neither overruns the buffer. Deadlock and its fix: T1 locks A then wants B; T2 locks B then wants A - a circular wait, so both hang. Impose a rule that every thread locks A before B, and the cycle cannot form, so the deadlock disappears."},{"heading":"Your turn","text":"1) Why can two threads each adding one to a shared counter leave it incremented by only one? 2) What property does a lock enforce on a critical section? 3) A semaphore initialized to 1 behaves like what? 4) List the four conditions that must all hold for deadlock. 5) Give one simple rule that prevents the circular-wait condition. Answers: 1) the read-modify-write interleaves, so both read the same old value and both write the same new value, losing one update - a race condition. 2) mutual exclusion: at most one thread inside at a time. 3) a mutex (a binary lock). 4) mutual exclusion, hold-and-wait, no preemption, and circular wait. 5) require every thread to acquire locks in the same fixed global order."},{"heading":"Common mistakes & exit ticket","text":"Mistake: assuming a single line like count = count + 1 is atomic - it is several instructions and can be interrupted mid-way. Mistake: adding more locks to fix races without ordering them, which trades a race for a deadlock. Exit: (a) A race condition is a bug whose outcome depends on what? (b) Which deadlock condition does a global lock-ordering rule remove? Answers: the exact timing or interleaving of the threads; circular wait."}]}'::jsonb, 'A race condition is a bug where the result depends on the exact timing of threads. The cure is to make the shared update atomic - all-or-nothing.', 15),
  (7, 'Checkpoint 2', 'quiz', '{"questions":[{"prompt":"With 4096-byte pages, the offset within a page occupies how many bits of the address?","options":["8","10","12","16"],"answer":2,"explanation":"4096 = 2 to the 12th, so the low 12 bits address a byte within the page."},{"prompt":"A page fault occurs when a program...","options":["divides by zero","accesses a page not currently in physical memory","writes to the disk","creates a new thread"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The hardware raises a page fault and the operating system loads the missing page from disk."},{"prompt":"The least-recently-used replacement policy evicts the page that...","options":["was loaded first","will be needed soonest","has gone unused for the longest time","is the largest"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Least-recently-used bets the recent past predicts the near future, so it drops the page unused longest."},{"prompt":"A race condition is a bug whose result depends on...","options":["the amount of disk space","the exact timing of interleaved threads","the display resolution","the file system format"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Different interleavings of the threads produce different results, which is the defining symptom of a race."},{"prompt":"A semaphore initialized to 1 behaves like...","options":["a mutex allowing one thread at a time","an unbounded queue","a page table","a scheduler"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Initialized to 1, the wait and signal operations let exactly one thread into the critical section."},{"prompt":"Which set lists conditions that must ALL hold for deadlock?","options":["paging, caching, aging, swapping","read, write, execute, append","fork, exec, wait, exit","mutual exclusion, hold-and-wait, no preemption, circular wait"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Deadlock requires all four together; breaking any one prevents it."}]}'::jsonb, null, 9),
  (8, 'File systems, I/O, and device management', 'reading', '{"sections":[{"heading":"What you''ll be able to do","text":"Describe how a file system turns raw storage blocks into named files and directories, explain the role of the index node and the directory, and outline how the operating system talks to devices through drivers, interrupts, and buffering. Warm-up: when you rename a file, does its data move on disk?"},{"heading":"The idea","text":"Storage devices offer only a flat array of numbered blocks. A file system is the software that turns that flat array into a tree of named files and directories. Each file has metadata - its size, permissions, timestamps, and the list of blocks holding its data - stored in an index structure commonly called an inode. A directory is itself a file whose contents are a list of names paired with the inode numbers they point to. Because the name and the inode are separate, renaming a file just edits a directory entry; the data blocks never move. This split also allows a single file to appear under several names, called hard links, since multiple directory entries can reference one inode. File systems must also survive crashes: if power is lost midway through an update, structures can be left inconsistent. Journaling addresses this by first writing the intended changes to a log, then applying them, so after a crash the system can replay or discard the log and recover to a consistent state. On the device side, the operating system does not talk to hardware directly from application code; it uses a driver, a module that knows one device''s command interface but presents a uniform read/write interface upward. Because devices are far slower than the processor, the operating system avoids busy-waiting: it issues a request and lets the processor do other work, and the device raises an interrupt when it finishes. Data is staged through buffers in memory, and frequently used blocks are kept in a cache so repeat reads avoid the device entirely. For bulk transfers, direct memory access lets the device move data to and from memory on its own, interrupting the processor only when the whole transfer is done."},{"heading":"Worked examples","text":"Rename versus move: renaming report.txt to final.txt changes one directory entry''s name while the inode and every data block stay put - which is why rename is instant even for a huge file. Path lookup: to open /home/ada/report.txt, the system reads the root directory to find ''home'', then that directory to find ''ada'', then that one to find ''report.txt'' and its inode - a walk down the tree, one directory at a time. Hard link: two directory entries, notes.txt and backup.txt, can point to the same inode; the file''s data exists once and is only reclaimed when the last name is removed. Interrupt-driven I/O: a program asks to read a block; the operating system issues the request and blocks the program; the disk works while other threads run; when the data lands, the disk raises an interrupt and the operating system wakes the waiting program. Cache hit: reading a recently read block is served from the in-memory cache in microseconds instead of milliseconds from the device."},{"heading":"Your turn","text":"1) What structure holds a file''s metadata and the location of its data blocks? 2) What does a directory actually contain? 3) When you rename a file, do its data blocks move? 4) What is the purpose of a device driver? 5) Why does the operating system prefer interrupt-driven I/O over busy-waiting for a slow device? Answers: 1) the inode (index node). 2) a list of names paired with the inode numbers they point to. 3) no - only the directory entry changes; the data stays put. 4) it knows one device''s specific command interface and presents a uniform read/write interface to the rest of the system. 5) so the processor can do useful work while the slow device runs, instead of spinning idly until it finishes."},{"heading":"Common mistakes & exit ticket","text":"Mistake: thinking a file''s name is stored inside the file''s metadata - the name lives in a directory entry, while the inode holds size, permissions, and block locations. Mistake: assuming the processor must wait while a device works; interrupts and direct memory access free it to do other work. Exit: (a) After a crash, what technique lets a file system recover to a consistent state by replaying a log? (b) What lets a device transfer bulk data to memory without the processor copying each byte? Answers: journaling; direct memory access."}]}'::jsonb, 'A file''s name lives in a directory; the file''s real metadata (size, location of its blocks, permissions) lives in an index structure often called an inode. The name and the inode are separate.', 14),
  (9, 'Virtualization, containers, and isolation', 'reading', '{"sections":[{"heading":"What you''ll be able to do","text":"Explain what a hypervisor virtualizes, contrast virtual machines with containers, and describe the isolation and least-privilege ideas that keep one program''s failure from becoming everyone''s problem. Warm-up: what does a virtual machine give a guest that makes it think it owns the whole machine?"},{"heading":"The idea","text":"Virtualization means giving software the illusion of hardware it does not exclusively own. A hypervisor is a layer that runs one or more virtual machines, each believing it has its own processor, memory, and devices. It works by the same trick as the operating system itself: it runs the guest in a less-privileged mode, and when the guest tries a privileged operation, control traps into the hypervisor, which emulates the effect safely. This gives strong isolation - each virtual machine runs a full, separate operating system, so a compromise or crash inside one is contained. The cost is weight: every virtual machine carries an entire operating system. Containers take a lighter path. Instead of virtualizing hardware, they virtualize the operating system: all containers share the host''s single kernel but each gets its own isolated view of processes, file system, and network through kernel features that partition names and limit resources. A container is therefore much smaller and starts far faster than a virtual machine, at the price of weaker isolation, since a flaw in the shared kernel can affect every container on the host. Underneath both sits the same principle that runs through this whole course: isolation. A well-built system gives each component only the privileges it needs - the principle of least privilege - and puts boundaries between components so that a failure or attacker in one part cannot reach the rest. Hardware modes, separate address spaces, file permissions, virtual machines, and containers are all mechanisms for drawing those boundaries at different levels."},{"heading":"Worked examples","text":"Trap-and-emulate: a guest operating system inside a virtual machine executes a privileged instruction to disable interrupts; because it is not truly privileged, the processor traps into the hypervisor, which decides what the guest is allowed to see and returns - the guest never notices it was not on bare hardware. Virtual machine versus container weight: running five services as five virtual machines means five full operating systems in memory; running them as five containers means one shared kernel and five slim isolated environments, which is why containers pack more densely and boot in a fraction of the time. Isolation trade: a security flaw inside one virtual machine is walled off by the hypervisor boundary, while the same flaw reaching a shared kernel could touch every container - stronger isolation costs more resources. Least privilege in practice: a service that only needs to read a configuration file is granted read-only access to that one file, so even if it is hijacked it cannot delete data or open network connections."},{"heading":"Your turn","text":"1) What does a hypervisor create, and what does each one believe about the hardware? 2) Name the mechanism a hypervisor uses when a guest attempts a privileged operation. 3) What key resource do containers on one host share that separate virtual machines do not? 4) Which offers stronger isolation, a virtual machine or a container, and why? 5) State the principle of least privilege in one sentence. Answers: 1) it creates virtual machines, each believing it has its own processor, memory, and devices. 2) trap-and-emulate: the privileged instruction traps into the hypervisor, which emulates the effect. 3) the host operating system kernel. 4) the virtual machine, because each runs a full separate operating system behind the hypervisor boundary, while containers share one kernel. 5) give every component only the privileges it needs to do its job, and no more."},{"heading":"Common mistakes & exit ticket","text":"Mistake: thinking a container runs its own operating system kernel - it shares the host kernel, which is exactly why it is lighter and less isolated than a virtual machine. Mistake: treating isolation as a single feature rather than a layered idea implemented by modes, address spaces, permissions, hypervisors, and containers together. Exit: (a) Do all containers on a host share one kernel or run separate kernels? (b) What is the name of the idea that a component should hold only the privileges it needs? Answers: they share one kernel; the principle of least privilege."}]}'::jsonb, 'A virtual machine virtualizes the hardware and runs a whole guest operating system; a container virtualizes the operating system and shares the host kernel. Different boundaries, different weights.', 14),
  (10, 'Comprehensive review quiz', 'quiz', '{"questions":[{"prompt":"A user program needs the kernel to perform a privileged action. It does this through...","options":["a context switch","a system call","a page fault","a hard link"],"answer":1,"explanation":"A system call is the controlled doorway from user mode into the kernel - see ''What an operating system does''."},{"prompt":"Compared with two processes, two threads in the same process...","options":["cannot run concurrently","share the same address space","each require a separate page table","cannot share any data"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Threads share the process''s heap, globals, and open files; only stacks and registers are private."},{"prompt":"Which scheduling policy minimizes average waiting time but risks starving long tasks?","options":["first-come first-served","round robin","shortest-job-first","strict arrival order"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Shortest-job-first runs the shortest ready task next, which is why long tasks can wait indefinitely without aging."},{"prompt":"In address translation, the page table maps...","options":["file names to inodes","virtual page numbers to physical frames","threads to cores","interrupts to drivers"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The page table records, per virtual page, which physical frame holds it, plus permission bits."},{"prompt":"A program spends almost all its time paging in and out instead of computing. This is called...","options":["thrashing","journaling","aging","buffering"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Thrashing happens when too few frames force constant paging - see ''Memory management and virtual memory''."},{"prompt":"Two threads run count = count + 1 concurrently and count rises by only one. The bug is a...","options":["page fault","deadlock","race condition","context switch"],"answer":2,"explanation":"The interleaved read-modify-write loses an update; the outcome depends on timing, which defines a race condition."},{"prompt":"Requiring every thread to acquire locks in the same global order eliminates which deadlock condition?","options":["mutual exclusion","no preemption","hold-and-wait","circular wait"],"answer":3,"explanation":"A consistent lock order makes a waiting cycle impossible, so circular wait cannot form."},{"prompt":"When you rename a file, what actually changes on disk?","options":["every data block is rewritten","a directory entry''s name, while the inode and data stay put","the inode number changes","the file is copied then deleted"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The name lives in a directory entry; renaming edits that entry and leaves the inode and blocks alone."},{"prompt":"The operating system issues a slow device request and lets the processor do other work until the device signals completion with an...","options":["inode","interrupt","offset","exec"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Interrupt-driven I/O frees the processor; the device raises an interrupt when the transfer is done."},{"prompt":"Which best contrasts a virtual machine with a container?","options":["a virtual machine shares the host kernel; a container runs its own","they are identical in weight and isolation","a virtual machine runs a full guest operating system; a container shares the host kernel","a container offers stronger isolation than a virtual machine"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Virtual machines virtualize hardware and carry a full guest operating system; containers share the host kernel, making them lighter but less isolated."},{"prompt":"Giving a component only the privileges it needs, and no more, is the principle of...","options":["least privilege","round robin","shortest-job-first","direct memory access"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Least privilege limits the damage a compromised or buggy component can do - see ''Virtualization, containers, and isolation''."},{"prompt":"The translation lookaside buffer exists to...","options":["store files on disk","cache recent virtual-to-physical address translations","schedule threads","recover the file system after a crash"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The TLB caches recent translations so the hardware avoids a full page-table walk on every memory access."}]}'::jsonb, null, 12)
) as v(pos, title, kind, body, notes, mins)
